  "body": "In general whether to run scripts in destroyed browsing contexts is kind of all over the place. In general it is possible, e.g. through code that stores a reference to a function from a same-origin iframe, removes the iframe, and then calls it. But, for example, unloading documents empties the document's list of active timers, so setTimeout calls scheduled before the iframe is removed will be canceled.\r\n\r\nBen Hsu from Mozilla was asking about the question of whether we should abort scheduled promise microtasks under these situations. Currently Firefox does so, but Chrome does not. I've asked him to comment here with a specific reproduction. (He provided [this](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/attachment.cgi?id=8804787), which does nothing interesting in Firefox and hangs Chrome causing the tab to die, but maybe we can get a more friendly one.)\r\n\r\nShould we consider doing so? How exactly would that work, in the spec and in implementations?\r\n\r\n/cc @bzbarsky, @rniwa, and I'll CC some Chrome folks internally",
